# GPL-2.0-only. Every source file (src/main.c, src/main-gcu.c, src/buildid.c,
# ...) carries the identical dual grant, reproduced verbatim in
# ATTRIBUTION.md and copied out of THIS tarball (there is no standalone
# COPYING/LICENSE file upstream, confirmed against both the release tarball
# and the GitHub license-detection API, which reports none): "(a) the GNU
# General Public License as published by the Free Software Foundation,
# version 2, or (b) the 'Angband licence' [a permissive-ish, non-standard,
# non-OSI-approved educational/research/not-for-profit grant]". We
# redistribute under option (a), the well-understood, unambiguous choice,
# matching the assessment's own GPL-2.0-only classification.
licence=GPL-2.0-only
licence_files=src/main.c src/main-gcu.c src/buildid.c
copyleft=aggregate
copyleft_note=Angband ships as a standalone /APPS/ANGBAND binary with its own corresponding-source obligation (this PORT file + patches/ + the exact upstream tarball are the source); it links userland/libc (MIT) and userland/ports/ncurses (X11-style permissive), neither copyleft, so nothing GPL flows into either of those. Same shape as VI's GPLv2 busybox in ATTRIBUTION.md, minus the LGPL regex complication (VI needed GPLv3 because it also links LGPLv3 GNU regex; Angband links nothing copyleft besides itself).

# THE ADOPTION TEST (owner rule 2): does this want to USE a platform layer, or
# to BE one? Angband USES. It is an ordinary Ring-3 program: it opens files
# under its ANGBAND_DIR_* paths, reads/writes a savefile, and draws through
# ncurses' terminal model. It owns no event loop anyone else must adopt, no
# window system, no service. The GCU frontend's only "own the process" moment
# is main()'s top-level game loop, exactly like every other curses program
# already ported (see ncurses' own adoption note): it calls INTO ncurses'
# getch()/refresh(), never the reverse.
adoption=USES. Angband is a self-contained Ring-3 curses program: it reads its lib/ data files and writes a savefile through ordinary libc file I/O, and draws through ncurses' terminal model (getch()/refresh()), never the reverse. It imposes no event loop, window system or service model on anything else; it is a leaf consumer of the platform and of the ncurses port, not a would-be platform.

# The first shipping port to exercise mports' `needs=` on a REAL dependency
# (the self-test's aaa/zzz pair being the only prior exercise, per
# docs/MPORTS.md's "Known limitations"). ncurses installs curses.h et al into
# out/include and libncurses.a into out/lib; both are required for main-gcu.c
# and the final link.
needs=libc ncurses

prepare=none

# See the port header above: adds src/mayteraos-curses-compat.{h,c} (a narrow-
# curses mvwaddnwstr()) and one #include line in src/main-gcu.c.
patches=0001-mvwaddnwstr-narrow-curses-compat.patch

